  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://radelement.org/ng/Pneumonia">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://radelement.org/ng/Diagnosis"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="Nd619a30a532e4321adf4fcf159df62d6"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="N34271a2121094347b60b4e8a58760b87"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="N05da84e2288b43e99a1f6219b60a4ea8"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="N494ca257e4e14f31901e13acb61d3732"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="N165f4453e1e541e7853edb249ca375db"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="N65baf9f5bba243f8ab06cb890e185c76"/>
    <rdfs:label>pneumonia</rdfs:label>
    <skos:prefLabel>pneumonia</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition>Infection of the lung parenchyma.</skos:definition>
    <cde:localId>DX-000016</cde:localId>
    <cde:nodeType>Diagnosis</cde:nodeType>
    <cde:hasBinding rdf:resource="https://radelement.org/ng/binding-Pneumonia-RADLEX-RID5350"/>
    <cde:hasBinding rdf:resource="https://radelement.org/ng/binding-Pneumonia-SNOMEDCT-233604007"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://radlex.org/RID/RID5350"/>
    <skos:exactMatch rdf:resource="http://snomed.info/id/233604007"/>
    <cde:anchorVerdict>anchored</cde:anchorVerdict>
    <cde:versionNumber rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</cde:versionNumber>
    <cde:versionStatus>proposed</cde:versionStatus>
    <cde:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-09-10</cde:created>
    <cde:lastModified rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-09-10</cde:lastModified>
    <cde:conceptSource>Fleischner Society</cde:conceptSource>
    <cde:sourceDefinition>Inflammation of the lungs. [MeSH]
Pathology.—Pneumonia is inflammation of the airspaces and/or interstitium (eg, due to infection, as in bacterial pneumonia). Infective pneumonia is characterized by exudate resulting in consolidation. The term is also used to refer to a number of noninfectious disorders of the lung parenchyma characterized by varying degrees of inflammation and fibrosis (eg, idiopathic interstitial pneumonias) (5).  [Fleischner Society]</cde:sourceDefinition>
  </rdf:Description>
